-- SOP-2.3.4 Vendor Approval, v4 -> v5: SQF 2.3.3 Contract Manufacturers is not applicable.
--
-- Owner's answers, 2026-10-17, from the FSMS Index (2.3.3.1 to 2.3.3.4 showed "No program"): no other
-- business makes or packs any product the site supplies, and the site arranges no third-party
-- storage or distribution - released product goes straight to the customer. A step and a short
-- paragraph saying so are added after the contract services paragraph, and the determination is
-- recorded in content.not_applicable (2.3.3, which covers 2.3.3.1 to 2.3.3.4) so the FSMS Index
-- shows the four clauses as Not applicable with this procedure. sqf_reference is NOT changed: a
-- document never cites a clause it rules out.
--
-- Approved by GJM, effective 2026-10-17.
--
-- Safe to run twice: at v5 as intended nothing is updated. Any other shape stops the migration.

do $$
declare
  d record;
  n int;
  proc_old constant jsonb := $j$["Suppliers are evaluated based on the type of product or service provided and their ability to meet food-safety and quality requirements.", "The following documentation may be requested when available:", "• Certificate of Analysis (COA)", "• Safety Data Sheet (SDS)", "• Allergen Statement", "• Certificate of Origin or Letter of Guarantee", "• Kosher, Organic, Gluten-Free, or Non-GMO documentation (if applicable)", "• Third-party audit certificate (GFSI, HACCP, or similar) or completed Vendor Questionnaire", "Suppliers that cannot provide full documentation (for example, Sysco or Restaurant Depot) may still be conditionally approved if they are recognized distributors supplying commercially regulated food products.", "The QA Manager / Owner will review supplier performance and documentation at least annually to confirm approval status.", "Supplier Status", "• Approved: Supplier meets all documentation or recognized-distributor requirements.", "• Conditionally Approved: Supplier lacks one or more documents but poses low food-safety risk.", "• Rejected: Supplier cannot demonstrate food-safety compliance or product traceability.", "> **Contract service providers are not approved through the steps above.** Pest control, chemical supply, laundry, waste haulage, calibration, external laboratory and sanitation providers supply a service rather than a material, and a certificate of analysis or an allergen statement asks them nothing. The description of their services, the training and licences their personnel must hold, and their contract are recorded on **FRM-206 Contract Services Register**, one entry per provider, as SQF 2.3.2.8 requires.", "Material from a supplier that is not approved is received only in an emergency, and only once the SQF Practitioner has authorised it.", "• An emergency is a supply failure that would otherwise stop or delay production: an approved supplier out of stock or unable to deliver in time, or a delivery rejected at receipt with no approved replacement available. Price, convenience and product trials are not emergencies — a supplier wanted for those is approved on FRM-202 first.", "• The SQF Practitioner authorises the receipt before the material is accepted, for the quantity needed to cover the shortfall and no more.", "• The delivery is recorded on FRM-301 like any other, with the Approved Supplier & COA/LOG/COC column marked N and the Comments recording EMERGENCY – NON-APPROVED SUPPLIER, who authorised it, and the result of the receiving inspection.", "• The receiving inspection is completed and recorded before the material is used: identity against the purchase order, the label and its allergen declaration against the material specification, packaging integrity, lot code and best-by date, and temperature where the material is chilled. Any COA, LOG or COC the supplier can provide is attached to the FRM-301 entry.", "• Material that fails, or whose inspection has not been recorded, is not used. Failed material is segregated, tagged HOLD – DO NOT USE on FRM-702 and dispositioned under FSQM-018.", "• An emergency receipt does not approve the supplier. If the supplier is to be used again it is approved on FRM-202 before the next order; otherwise it is not used again.", "Material from another site under the same ownership as Adventure Bakery is treated exactly as material from any other supplier.", "> Adventure Bakery has no other site today: no site under common ownership supplies it with ingredients, packaging or part-made product. The rule is stated so that shared ownership is never taken as a substitute for approval if one ever does. Such a site is approved on FRM-202, its materials are held to the specifications SOP-2.3.2 requires, and each delivery is received and inspected on FRM-301 like any other (SQF 2.3.4.5).", "A material bought through a recognised distributor is approved as a named product from a named manufacturer, not as whatever the distributor supplies under that description.", "• The brand on the pack is checked at receipt against the brand approved for that material, and recorded in FRM-301's Brand / Manufacturer column. A different brand — a distributor's substitution, or a different brand picked up in store — is a new material, even from an approved distributor.", "• Before a substituted brand is used, its allergen declaration, including any \"may contain\" statement, is checked against the specification of the material it replaces. If the allergens are the same, it is used and the substitution noted in the FRM-301 Comments. If they differ, it is not used until the SQF Practitioner has reviewed the change, because the allergen statement on finished product may have to change with it.", "• A distributor does not provide a certificate of analysis for each lot. For each material bought through one, the manufacturer's product specification or allergen statement is kept on file instead, as SOP-2.3.2 requires, and the per-delivery checks on FRM-301 stand in for the per-lot certificate."]$j$;
  proc_new constant jsonb := $j$["Suppliers are evaluated based on the type of product or service provided and their ability to meet food-safety and quality requirements.", "The following documentation may be requested when available:", "• Certificate of Analysis (COA)", "• Safety Data Sheet (SDS)", "• Allergen Statement", "• Certificate of Origin or Letter of Guarantee", "• Kosher, Organic, Gluten-Free, or Non-GMO documentation (if applicable)", "• Third-party audit certificate (GFSI, HACCP, or similar) or completed Vendor Questionnaire", "Suppliers that cannot provide full documentation (for example, Sysco or Restaurant Depot) may still be conditionally approved if they are recognized distributors supplying commercially regulated food products.", "The QA Manager / Owner will review supplier performance and documentation at least annually to confirm approval status.", "Supplier Status", "• Approved: Supplier meets all documentation or recognized-distributor requirements.", "• Conditionally Approved: Supplier lacks one or more documents but poses low food-safety risk.", "• Rejected: Supplier cannot demonstrate food-safety compliance or product traceability.", "> **Contract service providers are not approved through the steps above.** Pest control, chemical supply, laundry, waste haulage, calibration, external laboratory and sanitation providers supply a service rather than a material, and a certificate of analysis or an allergen statement asks them nothing. The description of their services, the training and licences their personnel must hold, and their contract are recorded on **FRM-206 Contract Services Register**, one entry per provider, as SQF 2.3.2.8 requires.", "Adventure Bakery uses no contract manufacturer, and arranges no third-party storage or distribution.", "> Every product the site supplies is made on its own site: no other business makes or packs any part of it. Released product goes straight to the customer, collected at the dock or shipped to them; storage or distribution the customer arranges after that is the customer's. SQF 2.3.3 Contract Manufacturers is therefore not applicable. If either changes, the change is assessed under FSQM-007 and this procedure is revised before the first such product is made, stored or shipped.", "Material from a supplier that is not approved is received only in an emergency, and only once the SQF Practitioner has authorised it.", "• An emergency is a supply failure that would otherwise stop or delay production: an approved supplier out of stock or unable to deliver in time, or a delivery rejected at receipt with no approved replacement available. Price, convenience and product trials are not emergencies — a supplier wanted for those is approved on FRM-202 first.", "• The SQF Practitioner authorises the receipt before the material is accepted, for the quantity needed to cover the shortfall and no more.", "• The delivery is recorded on FRM-301 like any other, with the Approved Supplier & COA/LOG/COC column marked N and the Comments recording EMERGENCY – NON-APPROVED SUPPLIER, who authorised it, and the result of the receiving inspection.", "• The receiving inspection is completed and recorded before the material is used: identity against the purchase order, the label and its allergen declaration against the material specification, packaging integrity, lot code and best-by date, and temperature where the material is chilled. Any COA, LOG or COC the supplier can provide is attached to the FRM-301 entry.", "• Material that fails, or whose inspection has not been recorded, is not used. Failed material is segregated, tagged HOLD – DO NOT USE on FRM-702 and dispositioned under FSQM-018.", "• An emergency receipt does not approve the supplier. If the supplier is to be used again it is approved on FRM-202 before the next order; otherwise it is not used again.", "Material from another site under the same ownership as Adventure Bakery is treated exactly as material from any other supplier.", "> Adventure Bakery has no other site today: no site under common ownership supplies it with ingredients, packaging or part-made product. The rule is stated so that shared ownership is never taken as a substitute for approval if one ever does. Such a site is approved on FRM-202, its materials are held to the specifications SOP-2.3.2 requires, and each delivery is received and inspected on FRM-301 like any other (SQF 2.3.4.5).", "A material bought through a recognised distributor is approved as a named product from a named manufacturer, not as whatever the distributor supplies under that description.", "• The brand on the pack is checked at receipt against the brand approved for that material, and recorded in FRM-301's Brand / Manufacturer column. A different brand — a distributor's substitution, or a different brand picked up in store — is a new material, even from an approved distributor.", "• Before a substituted brand is used, its allergen declaration, including any \"may contain\" statement, is checked against the specification of the material it replaces. If the allergens are the same, it is used and the substitution noted in the FRM-301 Comments. If they differ, it is not used until the SQF Practitioner has reviewed the change, because the allergen statement on finished product may have to change with it.", "• A distributor does not provide a certificate of analysis for each lot. For each material bought through one, the manufacturer's product specification or allergen statement is kept on file instead, as SOP-2.3.2 requires, and the per-delivery checks on FRM-301 stand in for the per-lot certificate."]$j$;
  his_old constant jsonb := $j$"v2 — 2026-09-16 — Cross-reference to FRM-206 Contract Services Register added, under D-10.\n\nFRM-206 was issued with nothing referring to it. The register stands on its own and appears in the Document Register, but somebody following this procedure had no way to reach it, and a register nobody is sent to is a register nobody fills.\n\nTHE LINE IS A CROSS-REFERENCE, NOT A SCOPE EXTENSION. This procedure's scope is suppliers of ingredients, packaging and cleaning chemicals, and its approval steps are a COA, an SDS, an allergen statement and a third-party certificate. A pest control company has none of those and should not be run through the list. The new paragraph therefore says what a contract service provider is not — approved through these steps — before saying where it is recorded.\n\nThe SQF reference on this document is unchanged for the same reason: 2.3.2.8 is satisfied by FRM-206, not by this procedure, and pointing at a register is not a claim to cover the clause behind it.\n\nv3 — 2026-09-16 — Form references pinned to document numbers.\n\nv2 added a numbered reference to FRM-206 and left two unnumbered ones beside it. One was merely vague — \"FRM Vendor & Supplier Questionnaire\" is FRM-203. The other was wrong in a way that matters: \"FRM Approved Supplier Register\" is REP-201, and it is not a form at all. It is a report projected from FRM-202's entries, with no entries of its own, so calling it an FRM tells somebody to go and fill in a document that cannot be filled in.\n\nThe same two documents appeared again in the records list under different loose names — \"Vendor Questionnaire\" and \"Approved Supplier List\", the latter not being any document's title. Both are pinned, because fixing one field and leaving the other would reproduce the inconsistency one line down.\n\nFRM-202, FRM-204 and FRM-205 are deliberately not added. They exist and they belong to this subject, but the body of this procedure does not mention them — it predates them — and a reference list should describe the procedure it sits on rather than the filing cabinet around it. Closing that gap is a rewrite, and it belongs to D-11.\n\nv4 — 2026-09-18 — Emergency receipt from a non-approved supplier, and material from sites under common ownership, under D-11.\n\nTwo Minor findings against this procedure, and one rule for distributors. 2.3.4.4: receipt from a non-approved supplier is acceptable only in an emergency and only after a receiving inspection is conducted and recorded — there was no route for it, so the procedure's \"whenever possible\" left the exception undefined. 2.3.4.5: material from another site under the same corporate ownership is held to the same specification, approval and receiving requirements as any supplier — there was no provision.\n\nTHE EMERGENCY ROUTE IS RECORDED ON FRM-301, NOT A NEW FORM. The receiving log already carries an Approved Supplier column and a Comments / Hold Status column, and its own instructions already say an unlisted supplier needs authorisation before acceptance. What was missing was the procedure those instructions point at: what counts as an emergency, who authorises, what the inspection covers, and that the material is not used until the inspection is recorded. A separate emergency-receipt form would be a second account of one delivery.\n\nAN EMERGENCY RECEIPT DOES NOT APPROVE THE SUPPLIER. Without that line the route becomes a side door: one emergency order and a supplier is in use indefinitely without ever having been approved.\n\nTHERE IS NO OTHER SITE. The owner confirmed on 2026-09-18 that no site under common ownership supplies Adventure Bakery. The rule is stated anyway, because 2.3.4.5 asks for the provision rather than the circumstance, and because shared ownership is exactly the relationship in which approval gets skipped.\n\nDISTRIBUTORS: A BRAND SUBSTITUTION IS A NEW MATERIAL. Most material arrives through Sysco and Restaurant Depot, already conditionally approvable here as recognised distributors. What a distributor's approval does not cover is the brand: when an item is out of stock the distributor substitutes, or a different brand is picked up in store, and a different cake mix can carry a different allergen profile. The new step makes the brand part of what is approved, checks it at every receipt, and holds a substitution with different allergens until it has been reviewed. It also states what stands in for a per-lot certificate of analysis, which a distributor does not provide.\n\nFRM-202, FRM-301, FRM-702 and FSQM-018 are added to the form references now that the body names them. The existing body, the Supplier Status list and the contract services paragraph are unchanged."$j$;
  his_new constant jsonb := $j$"v2 — 2026-09-16 — Cross-reference to FRM-206 Contract Services Register added, under D-10.\n\nFRM-206 was issued with nothing referring to it. The register stands on its own and appears in the Document Register, but somebody following this procedure had no way to reach it, and a register nobody is sent to is a register nobody fills.\n\nTHE LINE IS A CROSS-REFERENCE, NOT A SCOPE EXTENSION. This procedure's scope is suppliers of ingredients, packaging and cleaning chemicals, and its approval steps are a COA, an SDS, an allergen statement and a third-party certificate. A pest control company has none of those and should not be run through the list. The new paragraph therefore says what a contract service provider is not — approved through these steps — before saying where it is recorded.\n\nThe SQF reference on this document is unchanged for the same reason: 2.3.2.8 is satisfied by FRM-206, not by this procedure, and pointing at a register is not a claim to cover the clause behind it.\n\nv3 — 2026-09-16 — Form references pinned to document numbers.\n\nv2 added a numbered reference to FRM-206 and left two unnumbered ones beside it. One was merely vague — \"FRM Vendor & Supplier Questionnaire\" is FRM-203. The other was wrong in a way that matters: \"FRM Approved Supplier Register\" is REP-201, and it is not a form at all. It is a report projected from FRM-202's entries, with no entries of its own, so calling it an FRM tells somebody to go and fill in a document that cannot be filled in.\n\nThe same two documents appeared again in the records list under different loose names — \"Vendor Questionnaire\" and \"Approved Supplier List\", the latter not being any document's title. Both are pinned, because fixing one field and leaving the other would reproduce the inconsistency one line down.\n\nFRM-202, FRM-204 and FRM-205 are deliberately not added. They exist and they belong to this subject, but the body of this procedure does not mention them — it predates them — and a reference list should describe the procedure it sits on rather than the filing cabinet around it. Closing that gap is a rewrite, and it belongs to D-11.\n\nv4 — 2026-09-18 — Emergency receipt from a non-approved supplier, and material from sites under common ownership, under D-11.\n\nTwo Minor findings against this procedure, and one rule for distributors. 2.3.4.4: receipt from a non-approved supplier is acceptable only in an emergency and only after a receiving inspection is conducted and recorded — there was no route for it, so the procedure's \"whenever possible\" left the exception undefined. 2.3.4.5: material from another site under the same corporate ownership is held to the same specification, approval and receiving requirements as any supplier — there was no provision.\n\nTHE EMERGENCY ROUTE IS RECORDED ON FRM-301, NOT A NEW FORM. The receiving log already carries an Approved Supplier column and a Comments / Hold Status column, and its own instructions already say an unlisted supplier needs authorisation before acceptance. What was missing was the procedure those instructions point at: what counts as an emergency, who authorises, what the inspection covers, and that the material is not used until the inspection is recorded. A separate emergency-receipt form would be a second account of one delivery.\n\nAN EMERGENCY RECEIPT DOES NOT APPROVE THE SUPPLIER. Without that line the route becomes a side door: one emergency order and a supplier is in use indefinitely without ever having been approved.\n\nTHERE IS NO OTHER SITE. The owner confirmed on 2026-09-18 that no site under common ownership supplies Adventure Bakery. The rule is stated anyway, because 2.3.4.5 asks for the provision rather than the circumstance, and because shared ownership is exactly the relationship in which approval gets skipped.\n\nDISTRIBUTORS: A BRAND SUBSTITUTION IS A NEW MATERIAL. Most material arrives through Sysco and Restaurant Depot, already conditionally approvable here as recognised distributors. What a distributor's approval does not cover is the brand: when an item is out of stock the distributor substitutes, or a different brand is picked up in store, and a different cake mix can carry a different allergen profile. The new step makes the brand part of what is approved, checks it at every receipt, and holds a substitution with different allergens until it has been reviewed. It also states what stands in for a per-lot certificate of analysis, which a distributor does not provide.\n\nFRM-202, FRM-301, FRM-702 and FSQM-018 are added to the form references now that the body names them. The existing body, the Supplier Status list and the contract services paragraph are unchanged.\n\nv5 - 2026-10-17 - States that the site uses no contract manufacturer and arranges no third-party storage or distribution (owner, 2026-10-17), so SQF 2.3.3 Contract Manufacturers is not applicable, and what happens if that changes. The FSMS Index records the determination against this procedure. Nothing else in this procedure is changed by this revision."$j$;
  na constant jsonb := $j$[{"clause": "2.3.3", "reason": "Every product is made on site and released product goes straight to the customer: the site uses no contract manufacturer and arranges no third-party storage or distribution."}]$j$;
begin
  select count(*) into n from public.sop_documents where sop_number = 'SOP-2.3.4' and status = 'active';
  if n <> 1 then raise exception 'SOP-2.3.4: expected one active document, found %', n; end if;
  select * into d from public.sop_documents where sop_number = 'SOP-2.3.4' and status = 'active';

  if d.revision = 'v5' and d.content->'procedure' = proc_new and d.content->'revision_history' = his_new
     and d.content->'not_applicable' = na then
    raise notice 'SOP-2.3.4: already at v5 as intended, left alone';
    return;
  end if;
  if d.revision <> 'v4' or d.content->'procedure' is distinct from proc_old
     or d.content->'revision_history' is distinct from his_old or d.content ? 'not_applicable'
     or d.sqf_reference is distinct from '2.3.4, 2.3.2.8' then
    raise exception 'SOP-2.3.4 is not the document this migration was written against (rev %)', d.revision;
  end if;

  update public.sop_documents
     set content = content || jsonb_build_object('procedure', proc_new, 'revision_history', his_new, 'not_applicable', na),
         revision = 'v5', effective_date = date '2026-10-17', approved_by = 'GJM'
   where id = d.id;
end $$;
